		{
			"id": "MIgJFK7w",
			"type": "text",
			"x": 2658.458625613164,
			"y": 3541.3665183940902,
			"width": 1044.998779296875,
			"height": 125,
			"angle": 0,
			"strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
			"backgroundColor": "transparent",
			"fillStyle": "solid",
			"strokeWidth": 2,
			"strokeStyle": "dashed",
			"roughness": 0,
			"opacity": 100,
			"groupIds": [],
			"frameId": null,
			"index": "b5f",
			"roundness": null,
			"seed": 1095318196,
			"version": 6624,
			"versionNonce": 1821607692,
			"isDeleted": false,
			"boundElements": [],
			"updated": 1783726046045,
			"locked": false,
			"text": "When to Pick It: \nIt is the absolute gold standard for sorting Linked Lists, because linked lists don't require\nthe extra O(N) auxiliary array space that standard arrays do (pointers can just be redirected).\nIt is also heavily used in e-commerce or databases where predictable O(N log N) performance is\nstrictly required.",
			"rawText": "When to Pick It: \nIt is the absolute gold standard for sorting Linked Lists, because linked lists don't require the extra O(N) auxiliary array space that standard arrays do (pointers can just be redirected). It is also heavily used in e-commerce or databases where predictable O(N log N) performance is strictly required.",
			"fontSize": 20,
			"fontFamily": 8,
			"textAlign": "left",
			"verticalAlign": "top",
			"containerId": "TRFOBqsSTwNVohhq_-rDx",
			"originalText": "When to Pick It: \nIt is the absolute gold standard for sorting Linked Lists, because linked lists don't require the extra O(N) auxiliary array space that standard arrays do (pointers can just be redirected). It is also heavily used in e-commerce or databases where predictable O(N log N) performance is strictly required.",
			"autoResize": true,
			"lineHeight": 1.25,
			"hasTextLink": false,
			"link": null
		},
		{
			"id": "4DMBTG1C",
			"type": "text",
			"x": 2656.4977218970594,
			"y": 3807.525752899299,
			"width": 1065.4926288311299,
			"height": 125,
			"angle": 0,
			"strokeColor": "#e03131",
			"backgroundColor": "transparent",
			"fillStyle": "solid",
			"strokeWidth": 1,
			"strokeStyle": "dashed",
			"roughness": 0,
			"opacity": 100,
			"groupIds": [],
			"frameId": null,
			"index": "b5g",
			"roundness": null,
			"seed": 523748404,
			"version": 6873,
			"versionNonce": 1093059980,
			"isDeleted": false,
			"boundElements": [],
			"updated": 1783726046045,
			"locked": false,
			"text": "It is an \"out-of-place\" sorting algorithm. Sorting standard arrays requires an entirely separate\ntemporary array of size N to execute the merges. \nIf you are sorting an array with 10 million integers, you need enough RAM to hold 20 million\nintegers. \nFor pure array sorting, Quick Sort or Heap Sort are often preferred to save memory.",
			"rawText": "It is an \"out-of-place\" sorting algorithm. Sorting standard arrays requires an entirely separate temporary array of size N to execute the merges. \nIf you are sorting an array with 10 million integers, you need enough RAM to hold 20 million integers. \nFor pure array sorting, Quick Sort or Heap Sort are often preferred to save memory.",
			"fontSize": 20,
			"fontFamily": 8,
			"textAlign": "left",
			"verticalAlign": "top",
			"containerId": null,
			"originalText": "It is an \"out-of-place\" sorting algorithm. Sorting standard arrays requires an entirely separate temporary array of size N to execute the merges. \nIf you are sorting an array with 10 million integers, you need enough RAM to hold 20 million integers. \nFor pure array sorting, Quick Sort or Heap Sort are often preferred to save memory.",
			"autoResize": false,
			"lineHeight": 1.25,
			"hasTextLink": false,
			"link": null
		},
		{
			"id": "dEvxvgUDqOqE557tSZBgX",
			"type": "rectangle",
			"x": 2655.332544302624,
			"y": 3690.728403347289,
			"width": 1057.3389126899574,
			"height": 111.67013720532077,
			"angle": 0,
			"strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
